      "text": "The given rectangular coordinates are $(0, -3 \\sqrt{3}, 3).$ To convert to spherical coordinates, we need to find $\\rho,$ $\\theta,$ and $\\phi.$\n\nTo find $\\rho,$ we use the formula\n\\[\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}.\\]\nPlugging in the given values, we have\n\\[\\rho = \\sqrt{0^2 + (-3 \\sqrt{3})^2 + 3^2} = \\sqrt{27 + 9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6.\\]\n\nTo find $\\theta,$ we use the formula\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{x}{\\rho}.\\]\nPlugging in the known values, we have\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{0}{6} = 0,\\]\nso $\\cos \\theta = 0.$ Therefore, the angle $\\theta$ must be $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ or $\\frac{3\\pi}{2},$ since $\\cos \\theta = 0$ when $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ or $\\theta = \\frac{3\\pi}{2}.$\n\nTo find $\\phi,$ we use the formula\n\\[\\sin \\phi = \\frac{y}{\\rho}.\\]\nPlugging in the known values, we have\n\\[\\sin \\phi = \\frac{-3 \\sqrt{3}}{6} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\]\nTherefore, the angle $\\phi$ must be $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ or $\\frac{7\\pi}{6},$ since $\\sin \\phi = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ when $\\phi = \\frac{5\\pi}{6}$ or $\\phi = \\frac{7\\pi}{6}.$\n\nSince $\\rho > 0,$ $0 \\le \\theta < 2 \\pi,$ and $0 \\le \\phi \\le \\pi.$ Combining all the information we have, the spherical coordinates are $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = (6, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}).$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\boxed{(6, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6})}.$The answer is: 6,5\\pi/6,5\\pi/6",
